From: clasy...@gmail.com <Unknown>
Date: Tuesday, September 18, 2018 at 5:19:57 AM UTC-5
Subject: Description of the icons/shortcuts in the Setup Window
To: pepswin <Unknown>


As previously discussed, there is a Setup icon in the Commands window of 
PEPS.  When that is pressed, a window of setup options appears.  Attached 
is a copy of thus window which is described as follows.

Disable Auto-Arrange

This is a work-in-progress to enable the ability to disable the 
auto-arrange that is forced on all icon views as of Windows 7 as released.  
This is a useful feature to allow such as the default settings of the 
Commands window [and other windows such as the More Custom Programs 
window].  Note: Because of the limited nature of the Setup window, it is 
presented in 
Extended Tiles view, which may not be available in all Windows releases; 
however, this can only cause minor cosmetic differences.  Once any icon is 
selected, the full captioning text is revealed.  By using Extended Tiles 
where available, the entire text of all items present here is fully visible 
[which makes it easier to document[,  Extended Tiles view is highly 
impractical for more complex windows such as the Commands window, etc.

Various "scattered" registry patches exist that are not quite what is 
needed for PEPS, each solving incompletely some aspect of the problem.  As 
such, this feature will take some customization for each Windows release to 
be added as need be,

The program already knows how to identify Windows XP and Windows Vista; 
these systems require no modification, as the release elimination of this 
feature was accomplished in Windows 7.  However, the feature is not really 
fully removed, merely disabled.  [Many times, management on high issues 
edicts about things out of their personal preference, not that of users.  
Unlike some situations that were part of the downfall of DEC, these Windows 
programmers "fought back" in the sense that after-release "unauthorized" 
registry patches can be made that generally bring the feature back, albeit 
in some cases with some consequences.  Additionally, many of them are 
pointlessly tied to additional implementation of features beyond what is 
needed.  As such, the goal for a release version is to do as little as 
possible and document the problems that can be encountered.

In Windows 7, there is a weakness that cannot be "strengthened":  Unlike 
the desktop, a subordinate window, once modified to allow arbitrary 
placement of icons, can have the settings and placement destroyed by making 
further changes to the window, such as changing the overall screen 
resolution.  Worst  case, the icons have to be manually manipulated as is 
always the case once when setting up PEPS the first time.  Unless and until 
this is solved, it can always be a problem to be solved manually.  However, 
with the documentation of the recommended arrangement, intended order can 
be restored in a few minutes.

Once testing has been done on Windows 8 and 10, this feature will be 
finalized for release.  Testing allows a more automated program to 
accomplish what has been done [manually so far] for Windows 7,  The final 
version will make windows in releases past Vista be at parity with the 
older releases, etc.

Enable TC08 DECtape
For a variery of reasons, SIMH cannot simutaneously support the TC08 
DECtape controller and the TD8E DECtape controller.  Each has a different 
set of tradeoffs both in terms of the real hardware and as implemented in 
SIMH. For example, the TD8E actually takes more host X85 cycles.  Using the 
TD8E is thus primarily for supporting the TD8E directly, and otherwise is 
generally not recommended, etc.

Enabling the TC08 disables the TD8E and vice-versa.  PEPS implements 
environment control files to allow the various ,ini files to tailor SIMH as 
desired. Note: It is possible to have scripts that do not address any form 
of DECtape in which case these settings are moot.  Regardless, by changing 
both parameters at the same time simplifies the design logic of practical 
.ini files, etc.

Enable TD8E DECtape
This is the analogous inversion of the TC08 setting above,

Set Default Current Program File Name.
This is primarily a release option to allow the package to conform to 
existing documentation.  As documented, the default program filename for 
the Current Program File Name setting is HELLOWORLD.PAL.  The underlying 
BATCH script file can be modified to any other value if desired.

Set Default Editor to EditPad
Every component of the PEPS package concerned with a text editor has an 
internal override of this setting to force a specific editor choice.  
Assuming this setting is not applied, a check is made for the existence of 
an optional environment variable PEPS_EDITOR.  It this variable does not 
exist or is set to an unsupported value, an additional internal default 
setting will apply as a safety device.  The intention is to cause all 
"generic" editor situations to be equally affected by this setting to allow 
quick-change of the editor choice for personal preference reasons.

Note: Virtually all systems have Notepad [which may be the only choice].  
For this setting [for EditPad] to be viable, the program must be installed 
manually in portable install mode in the root if the same drive as the PEPS 
package is installed on.  [The portable version does not interact with any 
potential other fully installed versions.]  The usual installation is for 
EditPad Lite 7, which is the current release as of this writing.  This 
program is not provided in the PEPS package, but is widely available as 
freeware for non-commercial purposes, etc.

Note: Any change to the environment variables requires at least a full 
logoff to take effect.

Set Default Editor to NotePad
This is the analogous editor choice setting for Windows Notepad [the only 
editor provided in Windows].  There are various trade-offs in terms of 
editor choice.  Notepad is the only one where a new text file causes a 
separate window.  While tabbed editors may seem the right choice, this is 
not only personal preference, but arguably is not a universal result.  The 
number of tab changes for certain purposes may not be worth the effort for 
some specific commands, such as directory listings, which may be more 
useful using Notepad in that instance.  As such, certain editor uses are 
separately defaulted to Notepad and are not affected by this setting [or 
the other similar ones].  All underlying BATCH files can always be modified 
as required, etc.

Set Default Editor to NotePad++

This is the analogous setting for Notepad++, a popular text editor [also 
available in portable format as the same requirements apply as with EditPad 
as described above.]

Note: Regardless of the choice for the default editor, it may be desirable 
to force the use of Notepad++ when opening the SIMH logging file due to the 
presence of "unprintable" characters and potential file length.

Setup Log File
This manages the SIMH logging file.  Two questions must be answered 
[independently].  The session logging may be enabled or disabled; also, the 
logging file may optionally be cleared [which is recommended periodically].

Setup PEPS Logical Drive

The entire PEPS package requires an environment variable PDP-8_DRIVE to be 
set to "x:" where x is the drive to install the entire PEPS package on.  
[Values are thus such as "C:" or "D:".  The examples shown in the 
documentation assume installation on the P: drive.  [P as in PDP-8 and 
PEPS.]

To move to another drive, the entire package has to be copied to the 
intended drive; then this Setup option can be chosen from the moved copy, 
etc.

Note: When initially installing the PEPS package, since this is not setup 
yet, the batch script file INITIALIZE.BAT can be run from the root of the 
drive where the package is installed.  This is a special "private' shortcut 
to this setup option that does not depend on the prior value of the 
environment variable [and assumes it is not there].  The net result is the 
package will be setup as intended, etc.  It is not necessary to manually 
set environment variables; a one-page help file is included in this setup 
option since it is generally the first thing to be used when installing the 
PEPS package, etc.

cjl












